      "topic": "gs2_polity",
      "topicLabel": "DPSP & international peace",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "question": "Which section of the Indian Constitution refers to the responsibility of the state towards international peace and security?",
      "options": [
        "Fundamental Rights",
        "Directive Principles of State Policy",
        "Emergency Provisions",
        "Preamble to the Constitution"
      ],
      "answerIndex": 1,
      "explanation": "Article 51 under the Directive Principles of State Policy directs the State to promote international peace and security.\n\n\ud83d\udca1 DPSPs are like a parent's list of good-life advice for the state (be kind to neighbours, promote peace) \u2014 not enforceable in court, but meant to guide behaviour.\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f Preamble mentions 'international peace and security' isn't its wording; that phrase actually lives in Article 51 (DPSP), which is the more specific and correct answer.",
      "source": "MPSC State Tax Officer / Group B Gazetted",
      "year": 2016,
      "paperId": "mpsc-inspector-of-taxes-2016-gs-ii"
