      "id": "mpsc-tax-0038",
      "subject": "economics",
      "topic": "gs2_economy",
      "topicLabel": "Poverty line calorie norms",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "question": "Absolute poverty in rural areas is a condition where the calorie intake is less than",
      "options": [
        "2400 K calories per person per day",
        "2100 K calories per person per day",
        "2900 K calories per person per day",
        "2000 K calories per person per day"
      ],
      "answerIndex": 0,
      "explanation": "India's official poverty line calorie norm was 2400 kcal/person/day for rural areas (and 2100 kcal for urban areas, which require less physical labour on average).\n\n\ud83d\udca1 Think of it as two different daily 'fuel targets' \u2014 rural workers doing more physical labour need a bigger tank (2400 kcal) than city dwellers (2100 kcal).\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f 2100 kcal is a real number too, but that's the urban poverty-line threshold, not the rural one this question specifically asks about.",
      "source": "MPSC State Tax Officer / Group B Gazetted",
