      "answerIndex": 2,
      "explanation": "The Charter Act of 1833 ended the East India Company's remaining monopoly over trade with China and the tea trade, turning it into a purely administrative body.\n\n\ud83d\udca1 1813 took away the Company's monopoly over India trade but let it keep its China trade toy; 1833 finally took that toy away too, leaving it as just an administrator, not a merchant.\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f The Charter Act of 1813 is tempting since it already broke the Company's India trade monopoly, but China trade specifically survived until 1833.",
      "source": "MPSC State Tax Officer / Group B Gazetted",
      "year": 2019,
