      "explanation": "The Ilbert Bill Controversy (1883-84), over allowing Indian judges to try European offenders, occurred during Lord Ripon's viceroyalty and provoked fierce opposition from the European community.\n\n\ud83d\udca1 Ripon tried to make the courtroom colour-blind by letting Indian judges try Europeans too - white settlers reacted like a club being told its 'members only' rule was gone, and the backlash killed the bill's original scope.\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f Lord Curzon is a common wrong pick because he's associated with many controversial policies (like the Bengal Partition), but this specific one predates him.",
