      "subject": "english",
      "topic": "eng_grammar",
      "topicLabel": "Preposition usage",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "question": "Choose the appropriate preposition for the following sentence: The court has absolved him _______ all the charges leveled against him.",
      "options": [
        "of",
        "from",
        "to",
        "upon"
      ],
      "answerIndex": 0,
      "explanation": "'Absolve' pairs idiomatically with 'of': you are absolved of a charge, meaning cleared of it.\n\n\ud83d\udca1 Think of 'of' as peeling a charge off someone, like peeling a sticker labeled 'guilty' off their shirt.\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f 'From' feels natural since it suggests removal, but English idiom fixes 'absolve' to 'of', not 'from'.",
      "source": "MPSC State Tax Officer / Group B Gazetted",
      "year": 2025,
      "paperId": "mpsc-inspector-of-excise-&-narcotics-2025-english"
