      "subject": "history",
      "topic": "gs1_history",
      "topicLabel": "Protest against British repression",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "question": "Rabindra Nath Tagore gave up his knighthood because of -",
      "options": [
        "Chauri Chaura incident",
        "Execution of Bhagat Singh",
        "Simon Commission",
        "Jallianwala Bagh tragedy"
      ],
      "answerIndex": 3,
      "explanation": "Rabindranath Tagore renounced his British knighthood in 1919 in protest against the Jallianwala Bagh massacre.\n\n\ud83d\udca1 Tagore basically returned his 'medal' to British India in disgust after the Jallianwala Bagh massacre -- a symbolic slap in response to the killing of unarmed civilians.\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f Chauri Chaura and Simon Commission are other famous protest triggers but happened later (1922, 1928) and didn't involve Tagore's knighthood.",
      "source": "MPSC State Tax Officer / Group B Gazetted",
